-- ============================================================================
-- ERP database setup — complete schema for a FRESH database
-- ============================================================================
-- THE ONE SQL FILE A NEW INSTALLATION NEEDS. It creates every table, index,
-- unique key and foreign key the Spring Boot backend maps, so a database
-- prepared with it passes spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate (the default)
-- at first start: no missing-table and no missing-column errors.
--
-- WHAT THIS IS
--   The complete schema for a new/empty `erp_db`, built from the JPA entity
--   model (the source of truth) and reconciled with the incremental
--   docs/*_migration.sql scripts. Nothing is taken from a personal database
--   dump or backup.
--
-- WHEN TO RUN
--   New / empty database ONLY, once, BEFORE the first backend start.
--   It does NOT upgrade a populated database: CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS can
--   skip an existing table, and MySQL cannot add a newer column through it.
--   For an existing database apply the incremental scripts in
--   docs/database-deployment.md §5 instead (never this file).
--
-- NON-DESTRUCTIVE
--   Only CREATE TABLE / CREATE INDEX / ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT.
--   No DROP / TRUNCATE / DELETE / UPDATE and no data statement anywhere.
--   Reference data and the attachment-storage ledger row are seeded by the
--   application itself on first start (docs/reference-data.md).
--
-- HOW TO RUN
--   1) Create the database and the least-privilege application user once, as a
--      MySQL administrator (see docs/mysql-app-user.sql):
--        mysql -u root -p < docs/mysql-app-user.sql
--   2) Apply this file to that database as the application user:
--        mysql -u erp_app -p erp_db < docs/database_setup.sql
--      (MySQL Workbench: open this file, select the erp_db schema, execute all.)
--   3) Start the backend; `validate` must report no schema error.
--
-- REPEAT RUNS
--   CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS skips tables that already exist, so a partially
--   created database can be completed safely. MySQL has no IF NOT EXISTS for
--   indexes/constraints, so a second run reports "Duplicate key name" /
--   "Duplicate foreign key name" — those mean the object already exists.
-- ============================================================================

